  "narration": {
    "script": "How does a gas pump know when to stop? It doesn't. The [[emph +]] nozzle does. And it's not electronic. It's [[emph +]] air. See this tiny hole near the tip? Inside, rushing fuel squeezes through a narrow spot, creating [[emph +]] suction. That suction pulls air in through the hole, up a thin tube. While air gets in, fuel flows. When the tank's full, gas covers the hole. No more air. So the suction tugs a flexible diaphragm. That pulls a pin, a latch lets go, the lever drops, and a spring [[emph +]] snaps the valve shut. All from one tiny hole.",
    "voice": {
      "profile": "hold-up-narrator"
    }
  },
  "captions": {
    "style": "hold-up",
    "emphasize": [
      "hole"
    ]
  },
